================================================================================
(2) Is naive_clamp (GHRS ClippedReLU) a faithful [0,1] clamp in finite precision?
================================================================================
  paper example (fp16): x-1=2049 rounds to 2048, so naive_clamp(2050) = 2   (NOT in [0,1])
  ClippedReLU leaves [0,1] for 512 of the fp16 integers in [2,65504)
     stray values taken: [2.0]  ->  STEP maps them to [1.0]  (binary: STEP neutralizes them, so ClippedReLU still blocks THIS attack)
  clamp01 leaves [0,1] for 0 inputs  (provably 0, Lemma):  clamp01(1e4)=1.00, clamp01(inf)=1.00
  => ClippedReLU is not a finite-precision clamp; clamp01 confines every input
     to [0,1] unconditionally, independent of STEP tolerating out-of-range values.

